<article class="helios-journal-story"><a href="{{ routes.root_url }}?view=journal-world-of-helios-1">← World of Helios</a><h1>CARRYING THE MEMORIES OF AN ENTIRE CITY</h1><div class="helios-journal-story__body rte">
         

<div>Table of Contents 
  
</div>
<div></div>
<div><p>Memories of a place rarely exist as a complete map. They are preserved through small, sometimes very personal details, to the extent that for the same city, each person carries a completely different version.</p>
<p><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0644/2958/8701/files/mang-theo-ki-uc-cua-ca-thanh-pho-1.jpg?v=1789093042" alt="Carrying the memories of the entire city - Hoan Kiem Lake" loading="lazy" width="64" height="64"></p>
<h2>A city does not exist identically in everyone&#x27;s memory</h2>
<p>Iconic structures are often seen by millions. They appear in photos, on maps, and in countless narratives about a city. But the true value of an icon lies not just in its widespread recognition. It lies in its ability to become an anchor for very personal memories.</p>
<p>Hoan Kiem Lake is a landmark in Hanoi, but for those born and raised there, it might be an accustomed part of daily life. For those who studied, worked, then left Hanoi, it could be an image that draws them back to a certain period. And for someone who has only visited the city a few times, Hoan Kiem Lake might encapsulate the entire feeling of Hanoi in a very brief memory.</p>
<p>Ben Thanh Market is similar. It&#x27;s an easily recognizable structure, but what makes it endure in memory isn&#x27;t just its architecture. It&#x27;s in the rhythm of life around it, in the flow of people passing by, in the way many generations have used it as a landmark in a constantly expanding and changing city.</p>
<p>An icon, therefore, has two lives. One life belongs to the city, where it is part of its history and general landscape. The other life belongs to each individual, where the same image can be associated with entirely different stories.</p>
<p><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0644/2958/8701/files/mang-theo-ki-uc-cua-ca-thanh-pho-2.jpg?v=1789093048" alt="Ho Guom Ring - Preserving the ancient beauty of Hanoi" loading="lazy" width="64" height="64"></p>
<h2>Preserving the ancient beauty of Hanoi</h2>
<p>Hoan Kiem Lake is not just a landmark in the heart of Hanoi. Through layers of time, it has become part of historical memory and cultural life. From the legend of the returned sword, the image of Turtle Tower, Ngoc Son Temple, The Huc Bridge to the daily rhythm of life around the lake, all contribute to creating a space where past and present are continuously connected.</p>
<p>The Ho Guom Ring is inspired by this very depth. The ring doesn&#x27;t merely recreate a familiar scenic spot, but distills the visual and spiritual cues of Hoan Kiem Lake to transform them into a language of craftsmanship. The architectural layers, wave patterns, clouds, carved rhythms, and surface composition are designed to evoke a Hanoi that is trầm (serene, profound), deep, ancient, but not stuck in the past.</p>
<p>What the ring preserves is the value behind that image: history, memories, a sense of belonging, and a part of the identity that has shaped Hanoi through generations. The entire design is born from that inspiration, so that each detail does not stand alone but contributes to creating a complete impression.</p>
<p>For the wearer, the Ho Guom Ring can be a memory of their birthplace, or simply a connection to a Hanoi they cherish. But no matter where the story begins, the ring carries the same foundation: Hoan Kiem Lake is not just depicted on silver as an image, but from a relic becomes an anchor of the city; from a city, it expands into the roots of an entire realm of memories.</p>
<p><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0644/2958/8701/files/mang-theo-ki-uc-cua-ca-thanh-pho-3.jpg?v=1789093054" alt="Ben Thanh Ring - Carrying the vibrant rhythm of Ben Thanh Market" loading="lazy" width="64" height="64"></p>
<h2>Carrying the vibrant rhythm of Ben Thanh Market</h2>
<p>Some people are connected to Saigon because they were born here. Some come from elsewhere but grew up in this city. Others only lived here for a period and then left. Sometimes, all it takes is a place that has witnessed enough choices, enough new beginnings, and enough versions of oneself, for it to become an enduring imprint.</p>
<p>In that rhythm of life, Ben Thanh Market becomes a special identifying landmark: familiar enough for people to instantly think of the city, yet at the same time still situated within an ever-changing urban landscape.</p>
<p>The Ben Thanh Ring is inspired by this very spirit. The design goes beyond simply depicting the facade, tiled roofs, arched doorways, or the clock of Ben Thanh Market on silver as mere architectural details. These elements are restructured into a whole with a clearer, stronger, and more direct rhythm, reflecting the feeling of a city that is always moving, always opening up new connections, and not static in a fixed mold.</p>
<p>What&#x27;s important is that the Ben Thanh Ring doesn&#x27;t represent a check-in spot or an image to commemorate a trip. The ring is born from the spirit of the city: where many generations, many people, many rhythms of life continuously intersect to create a Saigon that is both familiar and new. Ben Thanh is merely the starting point for the image; what the product truly preserves is the modern spirit and vitality of the city behind that icon.</p>
<p><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0644/2958/8701/files/2aOboQxoAwzrvXbAkPGMii8jLdYzcdtgtJJbZCym.jpg?v=1789093421" alt="" loading="lazy" width="64" height="64"></p>
<p>A place can stand still. But the memory of it continues to move. From the imprint of the city to the imprint of a journey</p>
<p>HELIOS does not want these designs to merely reproduce landmarks. A familiar structure can make people recognize the product, but recognition is not the ultimate layer of meaning.</p>
<p>What HELIOS wants to preserve more deeply is the memory between people and the place they belong to.</p>
<p>The Hoan Kiem Ring can hold a part of Hanoi. The Ben Thanh Ring can carry a part of Saigon. The Vietnam pendant connects these imprints into a larger form.</p>
<p>There are places we pass through.</p>
<p>There are places that remain.</p>
<p>And there are places, even after leaving, that continue to travel with us.</p>
<p><strong>Carry the memories of a city. Connect the imprints on the same Vietnamese journey.</strong></p></div>
  

